> What you're suggesting probably won't fit well into the Nethack
> experience as it stands.  The AC system is simple and a bit backwards,
> maybe, but it works, and is easy to understand.
>
> Unless there is a kind of uber-armour that excels in all 3 areas (which
> would pretty much ruin your argument anyway) the practical offshoot of
> your suggestion is that the adventurer would have to lug around three
> heavy sets of armour in order to be ready for any eventuality, and
> waste time exchanging them between battles.  Rock-Scissors-Paper
> gameplay ensues.  Not fun.

Actually, I was envisioning using the various armor slots to balance
the three AC types.  So, get an undershirt (probably a new item, with
more padding than a t-shirt) to give blunt AC; some sort of plate to
protect against slashing, and a cloak against piercing; whatever your
boots/gloves/helm give is bonus.  Or whatever combination fits with the
equipment you come up with.

> Also, will enemies have different types of armour as well?  Will
> dragons be pretty much impervious to blunt damage but weak against
> piercing attacks?  That would be nice, for a little while.  Then we'd
> simply learn what is weak against what and murder everything in sight.

Wouldn't make much sense if the monsters weren't effected by it; would
also mean that different weapon types (most everyone uses swords &
sabers) could be useful.

> In Nethack, I feel that armour is already as context-sensitive as it
> needs to be by granting extrinsic attributes.  Black dragon scale mail
> is useful to keep around in case a black dragon show up, etc.
> Eventually, you pick the instrinsics you like most and stick to them,
> though.

Yeah, and the "standard AK" is fairly well sorted out: t-shirt,
[G|S]DSM, HoB, "o[LS|R], CoMR|Robe, Bo[S|L|WW], Go[P|D], =oFA,
=oConflict.  There's some variation, but not really all that much.

> By coincidence, this morning I was trying out Magic Monsters, a 7DRL
> that uses a system of blunt-slashing-piercing armour (as well as
> others) and so far seems to work well for me. (I seem to recall a good
> Playstation game called Vagrant Story that used a more versatile form
> of this system to great effect.)   Maybe the problem is that such an
> approach needs an entire game devoted to it.

Haven't played Magic Monsters, but I did make it about halfway through
Vagrant Story--which took this idea way too far.  Different elements,
different monster types, different types of damage, and you could make
your weapons strong against some, but weak against others; problem was,
you were severely limited in how many weapons you could carry, so it
was really hard to come up with a good set of weapons.  Far, far, far
too many variables. :P  If this idea has any merit, it needs to be kept
simple to avoid over-confusing the player; Vagrant Story is a good
example of how not to do it.

